 turn instead. 201 Next, notice that each agent has two cuts allotted in the offline bound of 2(k−1)cuts 202 (with the exception of the final agent, who has none). Thus, during each update, we can 203 simply remove the cuts corresponding to A1(resulting in at most 2(k−2)total cuts) and 204 add new cuts by rerunning the offline algorithm for the beads originally allocated to A1and 205 A2(returning to the bound of 2(k−1)). The only special case is when A1is not enclosed 206 withinA2and has no other adjacent agent at the time of allocation. In that case, we only 207 remove one of two cuts corresponding to A1, but we may add two cuts back. However, if we 208 add two cuts, we can note that A2only has one corresponding cut, so A1can ‘donate’ one 209 cut toA2, maintaining the property that each non-final agent has at most two corresponding 210 cuts. 211 Finally, we note that none of the steps in the update process violate the stated invariant 212 that the allocation is one that could have been generated by an execution of the offline 213 algorithm (up to order of interval selection), concluding our proof. ◀"

